  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Elevated fire weather conditions (hot, dry, breezy) lasting \n   into the weekend region-wide.\n\n-  Wildfire smoke degrading air quality and reducing visibility\n   for the next several days and likely well into next week.\n\nWestern Montana and north central Idaho will remain under\nsouthwest flow aloft with high pressure to our east and low\npressure to the west. This will maintain hot, dry and breezy\ncondition through the weekend.\n\nWindy conditions are expected to peak Saturday afternoon, with\nwidespread southwest winds gusting 20 to 30 mph. The combination\nof hot, dry, and breezy conditions will cause elevated fire \nweather concerns across the region. In addition, choppy conditions\non area lakes should be anticipated for those recreating on area \nlakes.\n\nThe southwesterly flow will also continue to transport regional\nsmoke from fires in Oregon and Washington into the region. Air\nquality will be diminished at times.\n\nForecast models are hinting at thunderstorm development as far\nnorth as Butte in southwest Montana and across Lemhi county\nSaturday afternoon and evening. The dry low levels of the\natmosphere will set the stage for high based storms capable of\nvery strong outflow wind gusts, possibly as high as 50 to 60 mph\nfor localized areas.\n\nThe upper level pattern changes very little for early next week,\nwith hot, dry and smoky conditions continuing. Monsoonal moisture\nwill remain predominantly suppressed to the south, limiting \nthunderstorm potential to occasional, slight chances across Lemhi \nCounty and southwest Montana. Forecast models do hint at high \npressure strengthening over the area by Wednesday/Thursday, which \nwould allow for further warming of temperatures and possibly for \nthicker smoke to settle into valley bottoms",
